counterfactuals.output:
Scarlett Keeling, a 15-year-old British girl, was found dead in shallow water on Anjuna beach, Goa, on 18 February 2008. She had spent the previous night at beach bars and had taken alcohol and drugs. Goa police initially treated the death as an accidental drowning.

Her mother, Fiona MacKeown, rejected that account and demanded another post-mortem examination. Subsequent medical findings documented numerous bruises and abrasions, evidence consistent with sexual assault, and signs that Scarlett was alive when she entered the water. Investigators alleged that she had been drugged and assaulted before being left to drown. Two local men, Samson D’Souza and Placido Carvalho, were arrested.

The concealment accusations arose because police reportedly:

- quickly declared the death accidental despite visible injuries;
- failed to secure the beach as a crime scene or promptly preserve evidence;
- initially minimized or omitted indications of assault;
- were slow to locate and question important witnesses;
- appeared reluctant to investigate people connected with Anjuna’s drug and nightlife scene.

MacKeown, some Goa officials, and sections of the Indian press argued that local police wanted to avoid exposing links among drug dealers, beach businesses, and allegedly corrupt officers—and to protect Goa’s tourism image. The police denied an organized cover-up, and some officials also criticized MacKeown for leaving Scarlett in Goa while she travelled elsewhere.

The case later passed to India’s Central Bureau of Investigation. In 2019, the Bombay High Court convicted Samson D’Souza of culpable homicide and related offences, while Carvalho remained acquitted. Thus, although serious investigative failures were widely alleged, the broader claim of a coordinated police conspiracy was never conclusively established in court.
